    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that formula makes sense. but honestly, the \"lead time variance\" part is what's killing us. sometimes a shipment arrives in 10 days, sometimes it's 35 days for no apparent reason. it feels like there are these random, unpredictable gaps in the data that just mess up the whole projection. how do we account for those extreme outliers in the math without making our safety stock levels massive and expensive?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "To handle those extreme outliers without inflating your safety stock unnecessarily, consider using a non-parametric approach or applying outlier detection methods before calculating the variance. Techniques like using the Interquartile Range (IQR) instead of standard deviation can provide a more robust measure of variability resistant to extreme values. Alternatively, if the outliers represent specific, known failure modes, model them separately, perhaps using a scenario planning approach for the worst-case delay.",
      "source": "target",
